-- title:
--   Multiplication by n>0 on J⁰ is locally quasi-finite
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a term $N$ of `JZeroNeronIdentityComponent p`: this bundles a scheme $G$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$ with structure morphism $g$, a relative group law $L$ on $g$ (functorial multiplication, unit and inverse on $T$-points $\{\varphi : T \to G \mid \varphi \circ g = t\}$, with associativity, unit and inverse laws and compatibility under base change), a bijection `pts` from $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$ onto the points of $g$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the algebraic closure of $\mathbf Q$ which is additive, Galois-equivariant and compatible with the Hecke algebra acting through endomorphisms of $g$ respecting $L$, commutativity of $L$, the hypotheses that $g$ is smooth, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact and surjective with preconnected fibres, the hypotheses that for every $n>0$ the morphism $L.\mathrm{schemeNsmul}\,n$ is flat and surjective, and a finite-index condition on the subgroup generated by the integral sections inside the Galois-fixed points; further fields are summarised here. Let $n$ be a natural number with $0<n$. Then the endomorphism $L.\mathrm{schemeNsmul}\,n : G \to G$ — the underlying morphism of the $n$-fold $L$-sum, defined by recursion from the unit point, of the tautological point $\langle \mathrm{id}_G\rangle$ of $g$ over itself, i.e. $[n]$ — is locally quasi-finite.
--
--   This records that multiplication by a positive integer on the identity component of the Néron model of $J_0(p)$ over $\mathbf Z$ has isolated points in its fibres; combined with the flatness and surjectivity already carried by the structure, it makes $[n]$ a quasi-finite flat epimorphism and the kernel $\mathcal J^0[n]$ a quasi-finite flat group scheme over $\mathbf Z$ (it is not finite, the fibre at $p$ being of toric type). It is used in the construction of the $p$-primary torsion core and of the torsion Hopf order whose fppf cohomology computes the relevant kernel.
